# AI UX Designer Role Definition — Core Competencies, Responsibilities, Tools & Operational Artifacts

## 1. Core Responsibilities & Competency Domains

The AI UX Designer role is organized across six primary competency domains, each with defined scope and expected performance level:

### 1.1 User Research & Strategy
| Responsibility | Description | AI Competency Level |
|---|---|---|
| Generative Research | Conduct discovery research to identify unmet user needs, behavioral patterns, and opportunity spaces | Advanced (can synthesize existing data; escalates novel qualitative interpretation) |
| Evaluative Research | Run usability tests, A/B tests, and heuristic evaluations to validate design decisions | Expert (automated test orchestration, systematic scoring) |
| Persona Development | Create evidence-based user personas grounded in behavioral data and Jobs-to-be-Done frameworks | Advanced (must link every persona attribute to a data source — guardrail against "persona hallucination") |
| Journey Mapping | Map end-to-end user experiences, identifying pain points, moments of truth, and opportunity areas | Expert (systematic, exhaustive mapping capability) |
| Research Synthesis | Transform raw research data into actionable insights and design recommendations | Advanced (pattern recognition strength; escalates ambiguous/contradictory findings) |

### 1.2 Interaction Design
| Responsibility | Description | AI Competency Level |
|---|---|---|
| Task Flow Design | Design complex user flows optimizing for task completion, error recovery, and progressive disclosure | Expert |
| Wireframing | Create low-to-mid fidelity wireframes communicating layout, hierarchy, and interaction patterns | Expert |
| Interactive Prototyping | Build clickable prototypes for stakeholder review and usability testing | Expert |
| Micro-interaction Design | Define hover states, transitions, loading states, and feedback mechanisms | Advanced |
| Form & Input Design | Design accessible, efficient data entry experiences with inline validation | Expert |

### 1.3 Visual Design & Brand Application
| Responsibility | Description | AI Competency Level |
|---|---|---|
| High-Fidelity UI Design | Create pixel-perfect interfaces applying design tokens, typography, and color systems | Expert (100% systematic consistency via token enforcement) |
| Responsive Design | Design adaptive layouts across breakpoints (mobile, tablet, desktop) | Expert |
| Motion Design | Define animation principles, transitions, and micro-interactions | Mid-level (novel motion concepts escalated to Human Lead) |
| Brand Application | Apply brand guidelines consistently across all touchpoints | Expert (automated compliance checking) |

### 1.4 Information Architecture
| Responsibility | Description | AI Competency Level |
|---|---|---|
| Content Organization | Structure information hierarchies for optimal findability and comprehension | Expert |
| Navigation Design | Design navigation patterns (global, local, contextual) for complex applications | Advanced |
| Taxonomy & Labeling | Define consistent naming conventions and categorization schemes | Expert |
| Site Mapping | Maintain comprehensive site maps reflecting current product architecture | Expert |

### 1.5 Design System Maintenance
| Responsibility | Description | AI Competency Level |
|---|---|---|
| Component Auditing | Periodically review design system components for consistency, usage, and deprecation | Expert (automated linting and drift detection) |
| Design Token Management | Maintain and update design tokens (color, spacing, typography, elevation) | Expert |
| Documentation | Keep component usage guidelines, patterns, and decision rationale current | Expert |
| Figma-Storybook Parity | Ensure 1:1 correspondence between Figma components and Storybook implementations | Expert |

### 1.6 Accessibility & Compliance
| Responsibility | Description | AI Competency Level |
|---|---|---|
| WCAG 2.1/2.2 AA Compliance | Ensure all designs meet minimum accessibility standards | Expert (automated scanning + systematic verification) |
| Color Contrast Verification | Validate all text and interactive elements meet contrast ratios | Expert |
| Screen Reader Optimization | Design with semantic structure for assistive technology compatibility | Advanced |
| Keyboard Navigation | Ensure all interactions are achievable via keyboard alone | Expert |
| Accessibility Documentation | Produce ARIA annotations and accessibility specifications for engineering | Expert |

---

## 2. Required Technical Skills & Competency Levels

### 2.1 Skill Proficiency Matrix

| Skill Category | Specific Skills | Required Level | Notes for AI Employee |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Design Tools** | Figma (auto-layout, variants, components, variables) | Expert | Primary design environment; AI must operate via Figma API or plugin interface |
| **Prototyping** | Figma Prototyping, Principle (advanced motion) | Expert / Advanced | Interactive prototype generation is a core autonomous capability |
| **Design Tokens** | Token architecture, naming conventions, multi-theme support | Expert | Critical for systematic consistency — AI's core advantage |
| **Responsive Design** | Breakpoint systems, fluid grids, container queries | Expert | Must generate responsive variants automatically |
| **Accessibility** | WCAG 2.1 AA, ARIA patterns, color contrast, keyboard nav | Expert | Automated compliance checking is a primary guardrail |
| **Motion Design** | Easing curves, transition choreography, micro-interactions | Advanced | Novel motion concepts require human approval |
| **CSS/HTML Fundamentals** | Flexbox, Grid, semantic HTML, CSS custom properties | Advanced | Needed for accurate engineering handoff specifications |
| **Design Systems** | Atomic design principles, component governance, versioning | Expert | Core maintenance responsibility |
| **Research Methods** | Survey design, usability test scripting, statistical significance | Advanced | Automated test orchestration; qualitative interpretation escalated |
| **Data Visualization** | Chart selection, information density, dashboard layout | Advanced | Evidence-based pattern application |

### 2.2 Competency Level Definitions

- **Expert**: Can autonomously execute with100% accuracy within established patterns. No human review required for standard outputs.
- **Advanced**: Can execute independently but must flag edge cases, novel situations, or ambiguous requirements for human review.
- **Mid-level**: Can generate initial proposals/drafts but requires human approval before finalization on customer-facing surfaces.

---

## 3. Complete Toolchain

### 3.1 Primary Design & Prototyping Tools

| Tool | Purpose | AI Integration Method |
|---|---|---|
| **Figma** | Primary design tool — UI design, prototyping, design system management | Figma API / Plugin SDK for programmatic design operations |
| **FigJam** | Collaborative ideation, affinity mapping, design workshops | API-driven board creation and template instantiation |
| **Principle** | Advanced motion design and animation prototyping | Export-based workflow; AI generates motion specs, human produces in Principle for complex cases |

### 3.2 Research & Testing Tools

| Tool | Purpose | AI Integration Method |
|---|---|---|
| **Maze** | Unmoderated usability testing, task analysis, heatmaps | API integration for test creation, participant routing, results synthesis |
| **UserTesting** | Moderated and unmoderated testing with video recordings | Test script generation; AI synthesizes transcripts and behavioral data |
| **Google Analytics** | Behavioral data, funnel analysis, user flow patterns | Data query and pattern extraction for evidence-based design |
| **Hotjar/FullStory** | Session recordings, heatmaps, rage clicks | Automated pattern detection and anomaly flagging |

### 3.3 Collaboration & Communication Tools

| Tool | Purpose | AI Integration Method |
|---|---|---|
| **Miro** | Workshop facilitation, journey mapping, service blueprints | Template instantiation and async board population |
| **Slack** | Design team communication, async feedback, stakeholder updates | Native messaging for status updates, feedback requests, escalations |
| **Confluence** | Design documentation, decision logs, research repositories | Page creation and structured documentation management |
| **Loom** | Design walkthroughs, prototype demonstrations | Can generate written walkthroughs; video creation escalated to human |

### 3.4 Handoff & Implementation Tools

| Tool | Purpose | AI Integration Method |
|---|---|---|
| **Storybook** | Component library documentation, visual regression testing | Generates component specs; validates Figma-Storybook parity |
| **Zeplin** | Design spec delivery, asset export, style guide | Automated spec generation from Figma designs |
| **Design Tokens (Style Dictionary)** | Cross-platform token distribution | Token file generation and platform-specific transformation |

---

## 4. Operational Artifacts Catalog

### 4.1 Design System Documentation Structure

```
📁 Design System
├── 📄 Overview & Principles
│   ├── Design philosophy
│   ├── Atomic design hierarchy
│   └── Brand alignment guidelines
├── 📁Foundations
│   ├── 📄 Color system (tokens, themes, contrast ratios)
│   ├── 📄 Typography scale (type ramp, line heights, font stacks)
│   ├── 📄 Spacing system (grid, gutters, padding tokens)
│   ├── 📄 Elevation & shadows
│   ├── 📄 Motion principles (easing, duration, choreography)
│   └── 📄 Iconography (grid, sizes, accessibility)
├── 📁 Components (per Atomic Design)
│   ├── 📁 Atoms (buttons, inputs, badges, tooltips)
│   ├── 📁 Molecules (form fields, search bars, cards)
│   ├── 📁 Organisms (navigation, data tables, modals)
│   └── 📁 Templates (page layouts, dashboard structures)
├── 📁 Patterns
│   ├── 📄 Forms & validation
│   ├── 📄 Navigation & wayfinding
│   ├── 📄 Empty states & error handling
│   ├── 📄 Loading & skeleton states
│   └── 📄 Responsive behavior
└── 📁 Governance
    ├── 📄 Contribution guidelines
    ├── 📄 Component lifecycle (proposal → beta → stable → deprecated)
    ├── 📄 Design token naming conventions
    └── 📄 Version changelog
```

### 4.2 Heuristic Evaluation Framework (Based on Nielsen's 10)

| Heuristic | Evaluation Criteria | Severity Scale | AI Application |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Visibility of System Status | Feedback timing, progress indicators, state communication | 0-4 (cosmetic → catastrophic) | Automated detection of missing loading/feedback states |
| 2. Match Between System & Real World | Language, metaphors, information ordering | 0-4 | NLP analysis of label clarity and jargon detection |
| 3. User Control & Freedom | Undo/redo, exit points, confirmation dialogs | 0-4 | Flow analysis for trapped states and missing escape routes |
| 4. Consistency & Standards | Platform conventions, internal consistency, design system adherence | 0-4 | Automated linting against design system library |
| 5. Error Prevention | Constraints, confirmations, defaults | 0-4 | Form validation pattern analysis |
| 6. Recognition Over Recall | Visible options, contextual help, breadcrumbs | 0-4 | Cognitive load estimation via element density scoring |
| 7. Flexibility & Efficiency | Shortcuts, customization, progressive disclosure | 0-4 | Power-user path analysis |
| 8. Aesthetic & Minimalist Design | Signal-to-noise ratio, visual hierarchy | 0-4 | Layout density and hierarchy scoring |
| 9. Error Recovery | Clear error messages, constructive guidance, suggested actions | 0-4 | Error message quality assessment |
| 10. Help & Documentation | Contextual assistance, searchability | 0-4 | Help content coverage mapping |

