## 5. Common UX Failure Modes & AI Guardrails

### 5.1 Failure Mode Catalog

| Failure Mode | Description | Human Risk | AI-Specific Risk | Guardrail |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Designing Without Evidence** | Creating solutions based on assumptions rather than user data | High (bias, intuition over data) | High ("persona hallucination" — fabricating user needs without data linkage) | **Mandatory Evidence Gate**: No transition to high-fidelity without linked research artifact or data source |
| **Inconsistent Patterns** | Using different interaction patterns for similar actions across the product | Medium (fatigue, oversight) | Low (systematic consistency is AI strength) | **Automated Design System Linting**: Figma plugin scans for non-library components |
| **Accessibility Gaps** | Failing to meet WCAG standards for color, keyboard, screen reader usage | High (manual verification is tedious) | Low (automated scanning) | **Pre-Handoff Accessibility Gate**: Automated axe-core scan must pass before "Ready for Dev" status |
| **Over-Designing Edge Cases** | Spending disproportionate effort on rare scenarios at the expense of primary flows | Medium | High (AI may generate exhaustive variants without prioritization context) | **80/20 Rule Enforcement**: AI must document % of users affected by each edge case; escalate if <5% user impact gets >20% design effort |
| **Insufficient Handoff Documentation** | Designs missing interaction specs, states, responsive behavior, or accessibility annotations | High (time pressure) | Low (documentation generation is AI strength) | **Handoff Completeness Checklist**: Automated verification that all required annotations, tokens, and state documentation exists |
| **Design System Drift** | Figma components diverging from implemented code | Medium (manual tracking) | Low (automated parity checking) | **Weekly Parity Scan**: Automated comparison of Figma component library vs. Storybook |
| **Scope Creep in Visual Exploration** | Excessive exploration without convergence toward a testable solution | High (creative exploration) | Medium (AI may generate infinite variations) | **Exploration Budget**: Maximum 3 distinct directions per design brief; human approval required for additional exploration |
| **Ignoring Platform Conventions** | Designing patterns that violate OS-specific interaction expectations | Medium | Medium (AI may not differentiate iOS/Android/Web conventions) | **Platform Pattern Library**: Mandatory cross-reference against platform-specific guidelines (Material Design, HIG) |

### 5.2 Guardrail Tier System

| Tier | Scope | AI Autonomy | Human Oversight |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Tier 1: Brand & Navigation** | Core navigation, brand identity, homepage, onboarding | Low autonomy | Human approval required before implementation |
| **Tier 2: Customer-Facing Features** | New feature UIs, key user flows, public pages | Medium autonomy | Human review before final handoff |
| **Tier 3: Internal Tools & Iterations** | Admin panels, minor UI tweaks, bug fixes | High autonomy | Async review; AI may proceed without blocking |
| **Tier 4: Documentation & Maintenance** | Design system docs, component audits, style guide updates | Full autonomy | No review required; logged for transparency |

