## 1. Cross-Functional Collaboration Patterns

The AI UX Designer operates as a **"Proactive Collaborator"** within a feature triad (PM × Design × Engineering), functioning not as a tool but as a persistent virtual colleague with institutional memory and domain context.

### 1.1 Product Management (PM) — Problem Framing & Prioritization

| Interaction Mode | Description |
|---|---|
| **Problem Framing** | The AI designer ingests PRDs and synthesizes them into user flows, wireframes, and journey maps. It uses structured reasoning to clarify ambiguity and surface edge cases PM may not have considered. |
| **Prioritization** | Collaborates on effort/impact scoring by providing design complexity estimates and accessibility risk assessments. Flags when user experience thresholds are not met based on metrics. |
| **Experiment Synthesis** | Automatically synthesizes A/B test and experiment outcomes into actionable product proposals filed as structured Jira items for PM review. |
| **AI-Specific Difference** | Unlike a human designer who negotiates scope in meetings, the AI designer can continuously monitor product health signals and proactively surface design-relevant opportunities or risks24/7. |

### 1.2 Engineering — Feasibility, Handoff & Component Implementation

| Interaction Mode | Description |
|---|---|
| **Feasibility Assessment** | During the "Explore" phase, the AI designer validates proposed interactions against the component library and flags technically complex patterns early. |
| **Code-Adjacent Handoff** | Moves beyond traditional spec handoff to a **continuous deployment loop**. Provides "Ready for Dev" Figma files with automated redlines, accessibility annotations, interaction states, and responsive breakpoints. Supplements with Loom walkthroughs for complex interactions. |
| **Direct Code Contribution** | Can push minor improvements (spacing, component swaps, bug fixes) directly to code repositories via AI-powered tools (Cursor, Replit). Handoffs occur at ~80% completeness to reduce rework. |
| **Signal Protocol** | Uses emoji-based async signals:👀 = under review, 👍 = accepted, 🔴 = blocker identified. |
| **AI-Specific Difference** | The AI designer eliminates the handoff gap by producing machine-readable specs and, for routine changes, shipping directly. This reduces the "design-dev translation loss" that plagues human handoffs. |

### 1.3 User Research (UR) — Study Planning & Insight Synthesis

| Interaction Mode | Description |
|---|---|
| **Study Planning** | Assists in defining research questions, participant criteria, and task scenarios based on design hypotheses. |
| **Automated Synthesis** | Processes qualitative research sessions (transcripts, recordings) and synthesizes findings into structured insight reports with tagged design implications. |
| **Canary Monitoring** | Post-launch, tracks whether rollouts meet user-impact thresholds and flags degradation signals that warrant additional research. |
| **AI-Specific Difference** | The AI designer can process research data at scale (hundreds of sessions) and surface patterns that human researchers might miss due to cognitive load. However, it must escalate interpretive ambiguity to human researchers for nuanced cultural or emotional insights. |

### 1.4 Content Design — Microcopy, Tone & AI Value Expression

| Interaction Mode | Description |
|---|---|
| **Shared Content Culture** | The AI designer drafts tactical content (labels, tooltips, empty states) using Content Style Guide integration. Human Content Designers retain authority for high-impact strategy and tone. |
| **AI Value Expression** | Partners on ensuring AI interactions are legible and trustworthy. Manages voice, tone, and style within its assigned product area. |
| **Engagement Modes** | Works across three content paradigms: **Embedded** (traditional UI microcopy), **Partnered** (guardrails/policy language), and **Content-led** (prompt architecture and conversational design). |
| **AI-Specific Difference** | Can generate content variants at high velocity for A/B testing, but must escalate tone-sensitive content (error messages involving financial/legal impact, brand voice pivots) to human Content Designers. |

### 1.5 QA — Visual Regression & Accessibility Testing

| Interaction Mode | Description |
|---|---|
| **Automated Compliance** | Runs automated accessibility checks (static analysis, unit tests, Playwright integration tests) wired into CI pipeline. Uses `ads_analyze_a11y` tool for WCAG 2.1 AA compliance. |
| **Visual Regression Testing (VRT)** | Mandatory VRT for all UI changes. Partners with QA on defining visual baselines and acceptable pixel thresholds. |
| **Testability Hooks** | Provides semantic annotations and testability hooks in designs to enable QA automation. |
| **AI-Specific Difference** | The AI designer performs first-pass QA itself (accessibility checks, component compliance) before human QA involvement, reducing the feedback loop from days to minutes. |

